# teademo: Tea program + TextInput widget under a real pty.
# Frame trace (DIFFERENTIAL repaints: each frame is ONE write; first paint
# hides the cursor; an unchanged line emits ONLY its \r\n advance, a changed
# line rewrites as \e[2K + text + \r\n):
#   init    \e[?25l \e[2K\e[7m \e[0m\r\n
#   'h'     \e[1A\e[2Kh\e[7m \e[0m\r\n
#   'i'     \e[1A\e[2Khi\e[7m \e[0m\r\n
#   \e[A    (NOTHING: ignored key -> UNCHANGED model -> runtime-owned
#            skip-render drops the whole repaint — the frame would have been
#            byte-identical.  Under a repaint this step wrote \e[1A\r\n.)
#   0x7F    \e[1A\e[2Kh\e[7m \e[0m\r\n
#   \r      \e[?25h + exit 0
# The single expect below spans the 'i' frame's tail DIRECTLY into the
# backspace frame's head: such a substring can only exist when the up-arrow
# wrote NOTHING between them — under a no-op-key repaint the old \e[1A\r\n
# churn would sit in between and the expect would time out.  So this asserts
# BOTH halves of skip-render: the no-op key emits no repaint bytes, and the
# changing update (backspace) still paints.
expect \e[?25l
send hi
expect hi\e[7m
send \e[A
send \x7f
expect hi\e[7m \e[0m\r\n\e[1A\e[2Kh\e[7m
send \r
expect \e[?25h
expect_exit 0
